    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5628?c=usm": {
      "title": "Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor of Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor of Biological Sciences | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor of Biological Sciences Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5628 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor of Biological Sciences The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor of Biological Sciences. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Please include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three professional references.  Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.  For inquiries about the position, please contact Dr. Mike Davis, Director of the School of Biological, Environmental and Earth Sciences (mike.davis@usm.edu, 601.266.4748). Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences at The University of Southern Mississippi is seeking a full-time, non-tenure track Visiting Instructor or Visiting Assistant Teaching Professor at the Hattiesburg campus to begin January 04, 2027.  This hiring appointment is for the Spring 2027 semester only.  The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate biology courses, including introductory biology and human anatomy and physiology. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate courses in biology. Collect and report assessment data. Participate in school, college, and university service activities. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications M.S. degree from an accredited college or university in Biology or closely related field. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Ability to effectively communicate biological knowledge in a classroom setting. Ability to implement activities that reinforce student learning in a classroom or teaching     laboratory. Ability to design and implement assessments to evaluate student performance and learning   outcomes. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. from an accredited college or university in Biology or closely related field. Prior higher education teaching experience as instructor of record. Proficiency with the Canvas Learning Management platform. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5622?c=usm": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar Cells (Gu group)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar Cells (Gu group) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Postdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar Cells (Gu group) Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5622 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Postdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar Cells (Gu group) The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Postdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar Cells (Gu group). Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division Polymer Science and Engineering Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded Yes Pay Grade Posting Close Date March 1, 2027 Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The\nPostdoctoral Research Associate in Organic Electronic Devices and Organic Solar\nCells (Gu Group) conducts research focused on organic electronic device\nfabrication, particularly organic solar cells, performance testing, and\noperational stability. This position investigates how materials, processing\nmethods, device architecture, and encapsulation affect photovoltaic performance\nand degradation under light, heat, and environmental exposure. The position\ncoordinates research and laboratory activities, analyzes and interprets data,\ncommunicates research findings to sponsors and collaborators, and prepares\npublications and presentations. The Postdoctoral Research Associate also\nassists faculty with sponsored project management and reporting and provides\nresearch guidance to graduate and undergraduate researchers. Primary\nJob Duties and Responsibilities Fabricates and optimizes organic electronic devices, particularly organic solar cells. Develops reproducible procedures for substrate preparation, active-layer and interfacial-layer processing, electrode deposition, and encapsulation. Investigates the effects of processing methods and device architecture on performance. Characterizes device performance using current-voltage, external quantum efficiency, and complementary electrical and optical measurements. Investigates performance-limiting mechanisms, establishes testing procedures, and evaluates reproducibility across fabrication batches. Evaluates device stability through operational and accelerated aging studies under controlled illumination, temperature, atmosphere, and humidity. Performs maximum power point tracking, analyzes lifetime metrics, and investigates degradation mechanisms and strategies to improve stability. Prepares peer-reviewed publications, research proposals, presentations, technical reports, and related documentation. Maintains experimental records, analyzes and interprets research results, assists faculty with sponsored project management and reporting, and communicates project progress to collaborators and funding organizations. Trains and mentors graduate and undergraduate researchers in device fabrication, characterization, stability testing, laboratory safety, and data analysis. Provides guidance in establishing research priorities and developing reproducible experimental practices. Participates in research collaborations, project meetings, conferences, and travel to collaborating institutions, national laboratories, or sponsor locations as needed. Adheres to University and departmental policies and procedures and supports safe laboratory operations and equipment maintenance. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D.\nfrom an accredited college or university in Materials Science and Engineering,\nPolymer Science and Engineering, Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Physics,\nElectrical Engineering, or a related field. Three (3) years of experience in\norganic electronic device fabrication and characterization. Three (3) years of\nexperience with organic semiconductor materials, thin-film processing, and\nmaterials-device performance relationships. A peer-reviewed publication record\nis required. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities (KSA) Knowledge of organic semiconductor materials, device physics, thin-film processing, and photovoltaic characterization. Knowledge of organic electronic device fabrication, characterization, and stability testing methods. Knowledge of experimental research methods and laboratory safety practices. Ability to design and conduct controlled research experiments. Ability to troubleshoot device fabrication, instrumentation, and measurement processes. Ability to analyze and objectively evaluate device performance, reproducibility, stability, and degradation data. Ability to maintain accurate experimental records and research documentation. Ability to conduct research independently and collaborate with faculty, scientists, engineers, students, and research partners. Ability to prepare scientific publications, research proposals, technical reports, and presentations. Ability to train and provide research guidance to graduate and undergraduate researchers. Ability to coordinate research activities, priorities, and sponsored project requirements. Skill in written and verbal communication Skill in technical writing and scientific presentations. Skill in organization, problem-solving, and data analysis. Ability to travel to conferences, collaborating institutions, national laboratories, and sponsor locations as needed. Preferred\nQualifications Three (3) years of experience with organic solar cells, including non-fullerene\nacceptors, is preferred. Three (3) years of experience with spin coating, blade\ncoating, slot-die coating, or related solution-processing methods is preferred.\nThree (3) years of experience with glovebox operation, vacuum deposition,\ninterfacial-layer engineering, encapsulation, current-voltage measurements,\nexternal quantum efficiency measurements, light-intensity-dependent\nmeasurements, or maximum power point tracking. Three (3) years of experience\nwith operational and accelerated aging studies, International Summit on Organic\nPhotovoltaic Stability (ISOS) protocols, and photoinduced, thermal,\noxygen-related, or moisture-related degradation is preferred. Three (3) years\nof experience with electrical, optical, or morphological characterization\nmethods used to evaluate relationships among materials, interfaces, device\nperformance, and stability. Three (3) years of experience with automated data\nacquisition, instrument control, or performance and lifetime data analysis. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5627?c=usm": {
      "title": "Lab Coordinator for the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences",
      "description": "Lab Coordinator for the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Lab Coordinator for the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5627 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Lab Coordinator for the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Lab Coordinator for the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade E10 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Please include a cover letter,\n curriculum vitae, and contact information for three professional\n references.  Review of applications will\n begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.  For inquiries about the position, please\n contact Dr. Brian Kreiser, Search Committee Chair, at (Brian.Kreiser@usm.edu,\n 601.266.6556). Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a\n full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not\n sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The Laboratory Coordinator is responsible for\nthe day-to-day management and supervision of General Biology and Human Anatomy\n& Physiology teaching laboratory programs. The position also ensures the\nmaintenance of a safe, organized, and effective laboratory environment. This\nposition is a full-time, twelve (12) month, renewable, non-tenure-track staff\nposition within the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences in\nthe College of Arts and Sciences. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Supervises teaching assistants in biology laboratory courses (BSC 103L, 104L, 107L, 110L, 111L, 250L, 251L), including promoting and enforcing laboratory safety protocols for both teaching assistants and students. Teaches one lecture section as needed, which may include courses such as BSC 103, 104, 107, 110, 111, 250, and 251. Manages key academic and administrative responsibilities, including enrollment oversight, addressing honor code violations, coordinating ADA accommodations, and providing student advisement as needed. Oversees the Biology Tutorial Center, including scheduling and supervising tutors, ensuring proper maintenance, and generating and maintaining required reports. Designs, develops, and evaluates educational materials for laboratory courses, including quizzes and examinations, and conducts weekly lab staff meetings to coordinate instructional activities and logistics between lab and lecture components. Assists in maintaining laboratory facilities, equipment, instrumentation, and chemical inventory to support instructional needs. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Master’s\ndegree from an accredited college or university in biological science or a\nrelated field. Must have eighteen (18) credit hours in biology. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, search\nengines, and web-based applications to support research, data analysis, and\ndocumentation. Ability to safely operate and maintain laboratory equipment, including autoclaves, pH meters, balances, microcentrifuges, microscopes, and heating/stirring devices. Knowledge of general laboratory procedures, tools, and supplies, including proper handling, calibration, and upkeep of instrumentation. Proficiency in the Canvas Learning Management System for course delivery, communication, and student assessment. Demonstrated ability to effectively deliver instruction and support student learning outcomes. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral\nfrom an accredited college or university in biological science or a related\nfield. One (1) year of experience in managing a laboratory environment. Two\n(2) years of experience in teaching undergraduate biology laboratories. One (1)\nyear of experience in course development and instructional design, including\nparticipation in teaching-related workshops or conferences. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5626?c=usm": {
      "title": "Project Manager for Voting Access Project, Roy Howard Community Journalism Center",
      "description": "Project Manager for Voting Access Project, Roy Howard Community Journalism Center | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Project Manager for Voting Access Project, Roy Howard Community Journalism Center Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5626 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Project Manager for Voting Access Project, Roy Howard Community Journalism Center The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Project Manager for Voting Access Project, Roy Howard Community Journalism Center. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division Journalism, Public Relations, and Advertising Position Type Staff Employment Status Part Time Grant Funded Yes Salary Range 15.00 - 15.00 Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date October 20, 2026 Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The\nProject Manager for Voting Access Project coordinates the planning,\nimplementation, and day-to-day activities of the Voting Access Project within\nthe Roy Howard Community Journalism Center (RHCJC). This position manages\nproject timelines, deliverables, partner coordination, documentation, data\nworkflows, community engagement activities, and publication support. The\nProject Manager-Voting Access Project works with reporters, university\npartners, consultants, data providers, and other project contributors to\ncoordinate activities and monitor progress toward established project goals.\nThis position also supports grant reporting and project evaluation by tracking\nmilestones, activities, deliverables, and project outcomes. Primary\nJob Duties and Responsibilities Coordinates project activities, timelines, deliverables, publication schedules, key milestones, and partner responsibilities to support the timely completion of project goals. Coordinates routine communication and project activities with partner universities, collaborators, consultants, and other project contributors, including meetings, interviews, partner check-ins, scheduling, and related logistics. Maintains centralized project documentation and tracking systems, including public records requests, reporting progress, shared files, meeting materials, datasets, project resources, and other required documentation. Coordinates project data workflows by organizing acquired polling-location data and related documentation and working with the project reporter, third-party data provider, and methodology consultant to identify trends, changes, anomalies, and potential reporting opportunities. Assists with preparing state- and county-level data resources to support reporting, fact-checking, digital presentation, and other project needs. Plans and coordinates community town halls and related outreach activities, including logistics, partner communication, programming, promotion, participant engagement, and other project-specific community engagement activities. Coordinates with editorial, data, and digital contributors on project publication and presentation activities, including story packaging, visual elements, digital components, and dissemination of major project findings. Supports grant reporting and project evaluation by tracking activities, milestones, deliverables, community engagement, and project outcomes. Monitors project progress, identifies potential delays, gaps, or coordination needs, and communicates necessary follow-up to Roy Howard Community Journalism Center leadership. Participates in project and editorial planning activities and represents the Roy Howard Community Journalism Center professionally during meetings, community events, partner activities, and other project-related assignments. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications One\n(1) year of experience with project and time management, managing multiple\nassignments, deadlines, and project components simultaneously. One (1) year of\nexperience working independently while collaborating with reporters,\nprofessional staff, university partners, consultants, and community\nstakeholders, including organizing information, maintaining accurate project\ndocumentation, and tracking progress across multiple workstreams. One (1) year\nof experience working with spreadsheets, datasets, shared documents, and\ndigital collaboration tools. Knowledge,\nSkills, and Abilities (KSA) Knowledge of project coordination, planning, documentation, and tracking practices. Knowledge of spreadsheet applications, data organization, and digital collaboration tools. Ability to organize and maintain accurate project records, datasets, tracking systems, and supporting documentation. Ability to identify patterns, inconsistencies, and trends within project data. Ability to establish priorities, manage multiple assignments and project components, and meet established deadlines. Ability to coordinate meetings, project activities, community events, and related logistical requirements. Ability to communicate project information, updates, action items, and next steps clearly and accurately. Ability to identify project delays, gaps, and coordination needs and communicate concerns appropriately. Ability to work independently and collaboratively with reporters, professional staff, university partners, consultants, community members, and other project contributors. Ability to maintain accuracy and responsibly handle project data, records, and information. Ability to learn and utilize project management, data, reporting, and digital tools necessary to perform assigned responsibilities. Skill in written, verbal, and interpersonal communication. Skill in problem-solving, organization, and time management. Preferred Qualifications One\n(1) year of experience in project coordination, research, journalism,\ncommunications, public policy, political science, data analysis, community\nengagement, or a related field. One (1) year of experience working with\nspreadsheets, databases, data organization, or research datasets; coordinating\nmeetings, events, research projects, or collaborative initiatives; and working\non projects involving multiple organizations, departments, or external\npartners. One (1) year of experience with public records, research methods,\ndata-informed reporting, election-related issues, journalism, civic\ninformation, elections, voting access, public policy, or community-centered\nresearch. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5621?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Biological Sciences | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Biological Sciences Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5621 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Biological Sciences The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Professor of Biological Sciences. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Please submit a cover letter, CV,\n Research Statement (up to 3 pages), Teaching Philosophy (up to 2 pages), and\n contact information for 3 professional references. For inquiries about the\n position, please contact Shahid Karim PhD, Professor Cellular and Molecular\n Biology and Search Committee Chair at 601-266-6232 or Shahid.Karim@usm.edu.\n Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and will continue until\n the position is filled. Upon completion of the hiring process, an offer of\n employment is contingent on a satisfactory background check. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a\n full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not\n sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The University of Southern Mississippi, Hattiesburg campus, invites\napplicants for a full-time (9 months), tenure track Assistant Professor\nposition in Cellular and Molecular Biology within the School of Biological,\nEnvironmental, and Earth Sciences beginning August 2027. A successful applicant\nwould contribute to the scholarship and teaching mission of the school. They\nwould develop an internationally recognized, extramurally funded research\nprogram. Applications from exceptional candidates will be considered in all\nareas of molecular, cellular, systems biology, cancer biology, immunology,\nvector biology, and infectious disease. Preference will be given to individuals\nwho show potential to integrate with existing institutional research strengths. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Develop  a robust, extramurally funded research program in an area of molecular and  cellular biology. Teach  undergraduate and graduate courses in line with area of expertise. Mentor  graduate students to further their professional development. Contribute  to the service mission of the University, college, and school by participating  in school, college, and university service activities. Advise  students in the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences. Perform  outreach to raise profile of institutional programs. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications A Ph.D. from an accredited college or university in molecular and cellular  biology or related field. Postdoctoral training. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Ability to produce high-quality  scientific documents in the form of manuscripts and grant proposals. Operation of instrumentation required to conduct research in their area of expertise. Preferred Qualifications Evidence  of exceptional scientific accomplishments such as publication of high impact  research in internationally recognized journals. Successful grant applications or other evidence of ability to secure extramural funds. Prior experience\nteaching at the collegiate level. About the School The School of Biological Environmental and Earth Sciences houses faculty\nwith diverse expertise that serves the most rapidly growing cohort of\nundergraduates at the University of Southern Mississippi. Currently, the school\nserves over 700 majors and 60 graduate students. The unit of Cellular and\nMolecular Biology is comprised of collaborative, research active faculty\n(https://www.usm.edu/molecular-cellular-biosciences/index.php) The unit\npossesses cutting edge infrastructure such as super resolution microscopy,\nbiosafety level 3 laboratories, and small animal facilities. Dedicated\nlaboratory space, a competitive startup package, and a salary commensurate with\nqualifications and experience will be provided. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5620?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Avian Biology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Avian Biology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Avian Biology Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5620 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Avian Biology The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Professor of Avian Biology. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants should submit a letter of application, curriculum\n vitae (CV), statement of research plan, statement of teaching philosophy, and\n contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin on\n December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Upon completion\n of the hiring process, an offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory\n background check. For inquiries about the position, please contact Dr. Carl\n Qualls, Chair of Search Committee, at (601) 266-6906 or Carl.Qualls@usm.edu. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a\n full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not\n sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth\n Sciences at The University of Southern Mississippi invites applications for a\n tenure track Assistant Professor of Avian\n Biology beginning August 2027. Research interests may include, but are not\n limited to, migration, vector biology,\n behavioral ecology, reproductive biology, pollination biology, and/or\n conservation biology. Ideal\n candidates will be expected to develop a highly interactive research program\n that synergizes with or enhances those of the School and the University. Preference\n will be given to individuals who show potential to integrate with existing\n institutional research strengths. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in line with area of expertise. Establish an active and externally funded research program that\nincorporates both graduate and undergraduate students. Mentor graduate students to further their professional development. Contribute to the service mission of the University, college, and school by participating in school, college, and university service activities. Advise students in the School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences. Perform outreach to raise profile of institutional programs. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications A Ph.D. from an accredited college or  university in Biology or closely related discipline. Publication record in peer-reviewed journals. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Ability to work effectively, collaboratively, and collegially with students, faculty, and staff. Strong written and oral communication skills. Operation of instrumentation required to conduct research in their area of expertise. Preferred Qualifications Postdoctoral research  experience. Strong record of scholarship demonstrated by high-quality publications in peer-reviewed journals. Record of seeking extramural research support evidenced by proposal submission and/or receipt of funding awards. Experience, commitment, and interest in teaching undergraduate and graduate level courses. About the School The School of\nBiological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences serves a large and diverse cohort\nof students and has strong research programs with 24 tenure-track faculty in\ncellular/molecular biology, organismal biology, ecology, geography, and geology.\nThe School operates a biosafety level 3 laboratory, an animal research facility,\nand a state-of-the-art instrumentation core that houses, among other equipment,\na confocal super-resolution microscope. The School also manages a 290-acre\nreserve, the Lake Thoreau Environmental Center, which consists of 100-year old\nlongleaf pine savanna, secondary pine-oak-hickory forest, spring-seeps,\nheadwater streams, and an 11-acre lake and is home to a facility that houses a\nherbarium, insect collection, and fish collection. Southern Mississippi exists\nalong a migration route for annual movement of bird populations between North\nand South America, and thus offers an ideal opportunity for research into their\nbiology, ecology, and conservation and interactions with other organisms. Dedicated\nlaboratory space, a competitive startup package, and a salary commensurate with\nqualifications and experience will be provided. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5619?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Elementary Education",
      "description": "Assistant Professor – Elementary Education | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor – Elementary Education Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5619 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor – Elementary Education The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Professor – Elementary Education. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Education Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position can be directed to Dr. Gretchen Goode (Gretchen.Goode@usm.edu). A completed application will include 1) a cover letter describing applicant strengths and qualifications for the position, 2) a CV, 3) the names and contact information of three individuals who can provide a recommendation, 4) a teaching philosophy, and 5) academic transcripts (unofficial are accepted but official transcripts are preferred and are necessary for hire). Application materials can be uploaded to the HR site found here: https://jobs.usm.edu. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The School of Education at The University of Southern Mississippi (USM) invites applicants for one (1) full-time, tenure-track, Assistant Professor position, beginning August 2027 with requirements to be present on campus. The research area of specialization for this position is open; preferred areas include math education, educational technology, or literacy education. Our faculty have established records of scholarship in culturally responsive education, multilingual learners, educational equity, multimodal composition, and inquiry, among others. We aim to recruit applicants who will produce impactful research and who will emphasize training to initial licensure candidates in the areas of elementary education to become technologically competent, culturally responsive educators who have an impact on the learning of P-12 students. Additionally, the successful applicant will effectively enrich in-service teachers attending our master’s program in Curriculum and Instruction. Qualified applicants will be prepared to contribute to the School of Education's research, service, and teaching missions at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Southern Miss combines the research opportunities of a Carnegie R1 university with the personal connections of a close-knit, interdisciplinary academic community. New faculty join a School of Education that values meaningful scholarship, excellent teaching, cross-program collaboration, and intentional support for faculty development. The School of Education’s Curriculum and Instruction and Elementary Education programs include bachelor's and master's degrees and include face-to-face, online, and hybrid delivery formats. Hattiesburg and the Gulf Coast campuses offer the cultural and educational amenities of a university city along with short commutes, affordable housing, historic neighborhoods, a strong local arts and restaurant community, and convenient access to New Orleans and other cities. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Developing a strong, independent research program. Collaborating with colleagues on program development and enhancement of pre-service and in-service teacher education programs. Teaching and mentoring students across programs. Seeking external funding. Contributing to the mission of the university and profession through service activities. Performing other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Doctoral degree in Curriculum and Instruction, Elementary Education, or a closely related field (must be completed by August 2027). Three or more years of full time K-12 teaching experience Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Demonstrated ability to deliver accurate, concise and quality communication (verbal and written) in a timely manner to a diverse population. Experience setting clear performance expectations and provision of meaningful feedback. Experience and/or familiarity with technology use, particularly in the online learning environment. Preferred Qualifications Teaching experience as a licensed educator in an elementary general education classroom. Demonstrated or potential excellence in scholarship and research. Experience teaching mathematics, reading, or other inclusive practices in elementary settings. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5617?c=usm": {
      "title": "Distribution Specialist",
      "description": "Distribution Specialist | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Distribution Specialist Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5617 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Distribution Specialist The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Distribution Specialist. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division Receiving and Warehousing Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Salary Range 11.85 - 11.85 Pay Grade N01 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Please Provide Cover Sheet with Resume. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary Distribution\nSpecialists are needed to properly and efficiently handle materials shipping to\nand from the University.  They are\nresponsible for receiving shipments, maintaining the warehouse, handling\nsurplus property, and assisting in property disposal. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Receives shipments, verifying\n     the number of packages received, noting the condition of packages\n     received, and collecting the proper documentation. Enters packages received and\n     delivered into the Track-pad system, obtains signatures as required,\n     and notes any damage or discrepancies. Performs duties such as\n     unloading and loading trucks for delivery, sorting items for delivery to\n     departments, delivering items promptly, and obtaining signatures as\n     required. Accepts and documents surplus\n     items received by organizing and storing surplus by categories, and maintaining\n     surplus records in the surplus property database. Processes requests for items to\n     be shipped out and maintains proper documentation. Operates vehicles such as vans,\n     trucks, forklifts, and pallet jacks. Maintains a neat and orderly warehouse.  Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications High School diploma or GED with at least three\nmonths experience in a warehouse atmosphere. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Excellent verbal and social skills. Ability to operate a computer, and possess a basic knowledge of\nMicrosoft Excel and Word. Able to lift 30 lbs. on a regular basis and\nwilling to work in all types of weather. Preferred Qualifications Associates\nDegree from an accredited college. Certified\nForklift operator. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5615?c=usm": {
      "title": "Classroom Therapy Assistant",
      "description": "Classroom Therapy Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Classroom Therapy Assistant Long Beach, MS, United States | req5615 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Classroom Therapy Assistant The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Classroom Therapy Assistant. Position Details Location Gulf Park 730 East Beach Boulevard Long Beach, Mississippi, 39560 United States Division College of Nursing GP Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Salary Range 12.71 - 19.87 Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary This role offers academic, social, emotional, and physical support to students enrolled at the DuBard School for Language Disorders. Serving as a classroom therapy assistant, the position involves working approximately 37-40 hours per week alongside a nationally certified speech-language pathologist, who delivers direct intervention to children with severe communication disorders and academic challenges. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Support the educational growth of students with significant communication disorders and academic challenges by working under the direction and guidance of the classroom speech-language pathologist Participate in implementing Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) for students and monitor their progress toward achieving IEP goals. Provide support for individual students inside and outside the classroom to enable them to fully participate in activities. Work alongside classroom speech-language pathologists to maintain student records, monitor progress, collect data, and prepare instructional materials. Support students with social, emotional or behavioral concerns and assist them in developing appropriate social skills. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Two years of college study (60 semester hours) is required. A minimum of one year of experience working with children in an educational setting is required. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Ability to utilize computer, computer software (word, excel, etc.), copy machine, fax machine. Ability to demonstrate sensitivity, respect, and discretion when dealing with confidential matters and student files. Ability to communicate effectively with children, colleagues, families, and university students. Ability to be compassionate and empathetic while working with a diverse population. Ability to be flexible in job assignments. Ability to learn the multisensory instructional method utilized in the program. Demonstrated skill with oral language and reading proficiency. Ability to successfully pass a background check. Preferred Qualifications Associate's degree from an accredited college or university or higher. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5618?c=usm": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Administrative Specialist Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5618 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/12/2026 Back to Search Administrative Specialist The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Administrative Specialist. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division College of Education and Human Sciences Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade N04 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary This position is for Academic, Operational, and Budgetary Support of the Dean’s Office, faculty, staff, and students. This position is essential as it is the first point of contact for anyone within the Dean’s Office, the public and university community. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Acts as academic support for the Dean’s Office to include, but not limited to, corresponding with prospective students, coordinating on-campus student visits, preparing and sending necessary student forms, maintaining appropriate student files and working with faculty, staff, students and university community to assure student success. Liaising with offices/departments/programs that offer courses through ISPD, e.g. all Student Affairs courses, Luckyday Scholarship Program, Center for Faculty Development. Assists with preparation of and inputs course schedule, room reservations and student scheduling needs. Manages all class enrollment permissions. Oversees textbook ordering. Organizes summer programs, including conferences. Develop and monitor the annual operating budgets through consultation with Dean, including planning and preparing justification statements for budget requests. Prepare analysis of budget data for the Dean and assure that expenditures stay within the established budget for the Dean’s office; monitors personnel accounts. Maintain and reconcile all budgets in a timely/monthly manner. Assist with Course Fee Audits. Assist university units and external agencies with budget requests. Prepare budgetary forms, and initiate processes in SOARFIN, obtain appropriate signatures and route to appropriate entity. (i.e. Purchase Orders, Permission to Travel, Reimbursement Voucher, Interdepartmental Invoices etc.). Initiates all Foundation non-scholarship withdrawal requests. Serves as facility and inventory point of contact. Maintain and track Dean’s office inventory. Conduct regular inventory audits. Assist in state mandated audits. Order course and lab supplies and coordinate delivery, as requested. Coordinate lab maintenance with off-campus technicians and appropriate lab coordinator or faculty. Assist with lab clean-out. Process necessary forms for office maintenance, supply purchases, awards, etc. through the University and Foundation. Serve as building liaison, enters work orders and interacts with physical plant on building maintenance and service. Manage personnel actions under supervision of the Dean, including creating job requisitions, onboarding and off-boarding of staff, faculty, graduate assistants, temporary non-students. Prepare appropriate HR hiring paperwork for faculty, staff, adjuncts, student employees, etc. Prepares credentialing paperwork for instructors. Hires, assigns duties, and supervises the work of student employees. Prepare semesterly paperwork for Graduate school, include tuition waiver requests, offer letters, and Personnel Action Forms. Provide administrative support for the Dean/Associate Dean’s including, but not limited to: gathering, analyzing, and/or inputting data; event planning and public relations; website management, calendar/scheduling and facilities reservations; correspondence, preparing reports for accreditation and funding agencies. Serves as back-up and support to fill in as needed by successfully completing cross training on all job duties and responsibilities outlined in this position; performs other duties as assigned by the Dean. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or general education degree (GED) and two years of office experience – OR- an equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Proficiency with a PC and keyboard to perform data entry, manipulation, and analysis. Ability to read and interpret documents such as safety rules, operating and maintenance instructions, and procedure manuals. Ability to write routine reports and correspondence in Microsoft Office. Ability to maintain cooperative relationships with colleagues. Excellent interpersonal, organizational, oral and written communication skills. Ability to exercise discretion, confidentiality, and judgment in dealing with sensitive issues. Ability to problem solve under direction of Dean and campus administrators. Preferred Qualifications Experience working in academia Minimum three years in a full-time office administration position Knowledge of SOAR, SOARHR, SOARFIN, Cornerstone, BlackBaud, University Foundation Go! System, Astra, and Faculty Enlight. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5612?c=usm": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in Polymer Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Professor of Practice in Polymer Science and Engineering | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Professor of Practice in Polymer Science and Engineering Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5612 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/9/2026 Back to Search Professor of Practice in Polymer Science and Engineering The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Professor of Practice in Polymer Science and Engineering. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Polymer Science and Engineering Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applications must be submitted online at https://usm.csod.com . A complete application will include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Brief statement of teaching philosophy that includes interest in specific courses in the polymer science and engineering undergraduate and graduate curricula and discusses approaches to laboratory, design, experiential learning, and integration of professional practice into the curriculum. Name and contact information for at least three references (letters will be requested following initial review). Review of applications will begin November 20, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. For inquiries, contact Dr. Derek Patton, Director, School of Polymer Science and Engineering at derek.patton@usm.edu . Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The School of Polymer Science and Engineering (SPSE) at The University of Southern Mississippi invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-track Professor of Practice position beginning August 2027. We seek candidates with strong technical expertise and professional experience in polymer science and engineering who will contribute to the School’s teaching, experiential learning, student mentoring, and industry engagement activities. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses in polymer science and engineering, with opportunities to teach graduate courses, particularly emphasizing laboratory, design, and other applied aspects of the curriculum. In this role, the successful candidate will draw on professional practice and applied experience to strengthen classroom and elevate experiential learning, while supporting student professional development and engagement with industry and other external partners. Opportunities also exist to engage in and support the broader research enterprise of the School, including participation in externally funded projects, applied research, workforce development, and industry-sponsored activities. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate and as appropriate, graduate courses in polymer science and engineering. Develop and deliver laboratory, prototyping, design-based, and other experiential learning activities. Advise and mentor students through capstone design, applied projects, and other project-based educational experiences. Mentor undergraduate students in academic, professional, and career development. Collaborate with faculty and staff on curriculum development, assessment, accreditation, workforce development, outreach, and industry engagement. Build and strengthen relationships with industry and government partners in support of education, experiential learning, workforce development, and collaborative activities. Contribute to grant writing, proposal development, and externally funded projects, particularly those involving applied research, workforce development, or industry partnerships. Contribute to the mission of the School, College, University, and broader professional community through service. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Earned Ph.D. in polymer science and engineering or a closely related discipline. Professional experience in industry, government, applied research, or a closely related setting relevant to polymer science and engineering. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to work effectively, collaboratively, and\ncollegially with students, staff, faculty, and external partners. Ability to apply polymer science and engineering\nfundamentals to practical problems and applications. Knowledge of common polymer characterization, processing,\ntesting, and laboratory methods. Ability to translate professional practice and applied\ntechnical experience into effective classroom, laboratory, and project-based\ninstruction. Ability to contribute to curriculum development, assessment,\nand continuous improvement. Strong written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Three or more years of professional experience in industry, government, applied research, manufacturing, or a related setting relevant to polymer science and engineering. Prior teaching, training, or student mentorship experience in higher education, industry, or another professional setting. Demonstrated ability to integrate professional or industry practice into teaching, experiential learning, and student mentorship. Experience with polymer processing, manufacturing, product development, scale-up, testing, or characterization. Experience advising design projects or multidisciplinary design-build-test activities. Familiarity with ABET accreditation and outcomes-based assessment. Experience contributing to externally funded, industry-sponsored, workforce development, or applied research activities. Demonstrated ability to develop productive relationships with industry or government partners. About the School The School of Polymer Science and Engineering (SPSE) is one of the nation's few academic units devoted entirely to polymer science and engineering. SPSE comprises 13 faculty members, including multiple early-career awardees. Research in the School spans polymer composites and materials for extreme environments, including fire-retardant and carbon–carbon systems; biomaterials and polymers for human health, including tissue repair, regeneration, and therapeutic delivery; materials for energy and sustainability; optoelectronic materials; and advanced coatings. SPSE maintains a robust, well-funded research portfolio supported by federal agencies, industry, and private foundations. The Thames Polymer Science Research Center provides state-of-the-art facilities for polymer research, including NMR (400/600 MHz), TEM, SAXS/WAXS, XPS/UPS, polymer processing (extrusion/injection molding), composite fabrication, and advanced thermal, rheological, and mechanical characterization tools. SPSE sustains one of the largest polymer-focused graduate programs in the country alongside a growing ABET-accredited undergraduate program, and offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in polymer science and engineering. For more information about SPSE, visit usm.edu/polymer-science-engineering . About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
