    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5582?c=usm": {
      "title": "Custodian II - Night Shift",
      "description": "Custodian II - Night Shift | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Custodian II - Night Shift Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5582 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Custodian II - Night Shift The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Custodian II - Night Shift. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division Administration Physical Plant Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Salary Range 12.50 - 12.50 Pay Grade N02 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Shift 10 PM - 6:30AM Job Summary Performs manual cleaning of the assigned building(s). Uses proper methods and materials in cleaning and care for the building area and equipment, participates in the care, cleaning, and general maintenance of the buildings that are assigned, completing all tasks including football, baseball, work orders, etc. as assigned. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Performs manual cleaning\n     of the assigned building(s). Uses proper methods and materials in cleaning\n     and care for the building area and equipment, participates in the care,\n     cleaning, and general housekeeping of the buildings that are assigned,\n     completing all tasks including football, baseball, work orders, etc.) as assigned. Must be able to show\n     knowledge on the following types of equipment:  commercial vacuum cleaners (uprights and\n     backpack vacuums), wet/dry vac, backpack blower, auto scrubbers, pressure\n     washer, shower machine, and carpet machines. Sweeps, mops, and scrubs\n     floors; cleans classrooms to include cleaning of desks and tables,\n     chalkboards, white-boards and erasers; washes windows, walls, blinds,\n     trash cans, air conditioning grills, glass and doors.  Cleans bathrooms, replenishes supplies,\n     and cleans drinking fountains in assigned buildings. Cleans and polishes\n     brass and stainless steel. Empties trash inside and outside buildings,\n     sweeps entrances and outside steps, ramps, sidewalks, and cleans buildings\n     signs. Cleans entrances (including windows, door areas, and entrance\n     mats), sweeps and mops stairwells, vacuums carpeted areas. Cleans large public areas\n     such as football stadium, basketball coliseum, baseball stadium, and Bennett\n     Auditorium. Cleans areas used for special events on campus before and\n     after the events. Unlocks buildings and\n     cleans other areas as assigned in coverage zones. Responds to emergency\n     situations during and after hours. Emergency situations may\n     include; property damage, personal injury, water leaks, flooding,\n     overflowing restroom fixtures, broken window or door glass, and\n     accidents resulting in the appearance of bodily fluids (blood, vomit\n     waste, and urine). Serves as a member of the\n     Physical Plant Essential Personnel and as directed by the Physical Plant\n     participates in pre-emergency preparations and post emergency recovery\n     efforts. Additional duties as\n     assigned by Custodial Leadership. Minimum Qualifications High School diploma or general education degree (GED). Six (6) months of related experience in a higher education setting or commercial cleaning environment. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge of University policies and procedures, professional standards and regulations. Knowledge of cleaning techniques and procedures, and the use of custodial materials, tools, equipment, and chemicals. Skill in managing ones’ own time while completing assignments accurately and with attention to detail. Ability to listen to, understand, and follow instructions. Ability to handle difficult and stressful situations with professional composure. Ability to react and respond quickly and calmly in emergency situations. Ability to establish and maintain a good rapport with university faculty and staff, students, and the general public. Ability to work successfully as a member of a team and independently with moderate supervision. Ability to operate personal computer with a general understanding of application software, and an understanding of the internet. Ability to work evenings, nights, and weekends as necessary. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5578?c=usm": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow--Karim Lab",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow--Karim Lab | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Postdoctoral Fellow--Karim Lab Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5578 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/1/2026 Back to Search Postdoctoral Fellow--Karim Lab The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Postdoctoral Fellow--Karim Lab. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Biological, Environmental, and Earth Sciences Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded Yes Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Review of applications will begin\n  immediately and will continue until the position is filled.  Applicants should submit a cover letter\n  detailing qualifications and relevant experience, a CV, and contact\n  information for 3 professional references. \n  For inquiries about the position, please contact Dr. Shahid Karim at\n  Shahid.Karim@usm.edu. Applicants must be currently authorized\n to work in the United States on a full-time basis without the need for visa\n sponsorship. The University will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g.,\n H-1B). Job Summary The Postdoctoral Fellow conducts scientific\nresearch in the Karim Laboratory focused on Alpha-Gal Syndrome (AGS), a tick\nbite-induced meat allergy of public health significance. This position\ncollaborates with the Principal Investigator and research team to design and\nconduct research using molecular biology, immunology, and functional genomics\napproaches to investigate how components of tick saliva contribute to the\ndevelopment of AGS. The Postdoctoral Fellow conducts laboratory experiments,\nmaintains tick colonies, analyzes and documents research data, and contributes\nto manuscripts, grant applications, and scientific presentations. This is a\nrestricted position with no defined end date. Continued employment is dependent\nupon project needs, availability of funding, and satisfactory performance. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Collaborates with the Principal Investigator and research team to plan, design, and execute scientific research projects related to Alpha-Gal Syndrome and tick-host interactions. Conducts laboratory experiments using molecular biology, immunology, functional genomics, and other appropriate research methods to investigate tick bite-induced meat allergy. Maintains and manages laboratory tick colonies in accordance with established research protocols and laboratory procedures. Maintains accurate and detailed laboratory notebooks, research data, experimental records, and other required research documentation. Analyzes and interprets research findings and contributes to the preparation of data, figures, and other materials used to communicate research results. Drafts and revises scientific manuscripts, abstracts, reports, and other research materials for publication and dissemination. Prepares and contributes research data and scientific content for extramural grant applications and presents research findings at scientific conferences and other professional meetings. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D.\nor DVM/Ph.D. from an accredited college or university in Immunology, Molecular\nBiology, Computational Biology, Biomedical Sciences, Biological Sciences, or a\nrelated field. Two (2) years of experience conducting research involving ticks\nand animal models. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge of immunology, molecular biology, computational biology, and functional genomics principles and research methods. Knowledge of tick biology, host immunology, and immune response pathways. Knowledge of transcriptomic analysis and its application to biomedical research. Knowledge of immunological assays and methods used to evaluate host immune responses to arthropod vectors and pathogen exposure. Knowledge of laboratory\nresearch methods, experimental design, data collection, and research\ndocumentation practices. Ability to conduct, analyze, and interpret scientific research and experimental data. Ability to maintain accurate laboratory notebooks, research records, datasets, and supporting documentation. Ability to troubleshoot laboratory, experimental, and technical challenges. Ability to prepare scientific manuscripts, abstracts, research reports, grant-related materials, and professional presentations. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a research team. Ability to organize and prioritize multiple research activities and meet established deadlines. Skill in written, verbal, and scientific communication. Skill in the use of Microsoft Office applications and research data management practices. Preferred Qualifications Five (5) years of\nexperience conducting research involving Amblyomma ticks. One\n(1) year of experience cultivating tick-borne pathogens in cell culture. Two\n(2) years of experience conducting immunological assays used to evaluate host\nimmune responses to arthropod vectors or pathogen exposure. One (1) year of experience\nconducting transcriptomic analyses related to immune response pathways. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
