    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5613?c=usm": {
      "title": "Aquaculture Technician I",
      "description": "Aquaculture Technician I | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Aquaculture Technician I Ocean Springs, MS, United States | req5613 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Aquaculture Technician I The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Aquaculture Technician I. Position Details Location Gulf Coast Research Laboratory 703 East Beach Drive Ocean Springs, Mississippi, 39564 United States Division Thad Cochran Marine Aquacultural Center Position Type Staff Employment Status Part Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently\nauthorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis without the need\nfor visa sponsorship. The University will not sponsor applicants for work visas\n(e.g., H-1B). Job Summary This is entry-level technical\nwork in recirculating aquaculture systems with field work required.  Characteristic duties\ninclude operation and maintenance of recirculating\naquaculture systems.  Additional duties\ninclude the collection, preparation, and maintenance of field and\nlaboratory equipment, specimens, and data.  The\nincumbent works with shellfish, finfish, algae and other marine species to rear\nlarvae and seed for stock enhancement and commercial scale grow out. Operations\ninclude broodstock management, larval husbandry, health management and\noperation of recirculating aquaculture systems. Work is performed under the direct supervision of a\nbiologist, scientist or an administrative or technical superior. Interaction with faculty,\nstaff and students on various projects will be required.  This assignment is\nanticipated to work 19 hours or less per week as a part-time employee without\nbenefits. The position will be located at the Thad Cochran\nMarine Aquaculture Center in Ocean Springs, MS. The Center is part of the Gulf\nCoast Research Laboratory (GCRL), a unit of the University of Southern\nMississippi. The Center is located on the Cedar Point campus of GCRL, a\n225-acre site devoted primarily to marine aquaculture. The Center currently\nincludes approximately 100,000 square feet of culture space devoted to live\nfeeds, broodstock, hatchery, nursery/growout, laboratory, experimental, and\nanalytical facilities. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Performs custodial and maintenance activities of physical facilities, structures, grounds, aquaculture tanks, mechanical and water systems, water control structures and equipment under supervision. Assists in the propagation and rearing of aquatic species. Performs various sampling activities including counting, sorting, measuring, weighing, and grading of samples. Provides detailed records and data entry for monitoring parameters and production data to support specific research or management projects. Participates in informational and educational outreach programs. Follows standard operating procedures and performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Associate degree from an\naccredited community college, technical college, vocational school or\nuniversity in biology, chemistry, aquaculture, fisheries, engineering, or\nrelated field. A valid Driver’s License is required. OR Graduation from a standard four-year high school or\nequivalent (GED or High School Equivalency Diploma) and one (1) year of related\nwork experience. A valid Driver’s License is required. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Proficient use of personal computers and software for word processing, presentation development, databases, and statistical applications. Ability to take detailed directions and work independently or as a team member. Ability to be proactive, resourceful and self-motivated. Demonstrate excellent organization and time management skills. Proficient interpersonal and communication skills both written and verbal form. Ability\nto repeatedly lift and carry weights more than 30 lbs. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5602?c=usm": {
      "title": "Marine Research Technician",
      "description": "Marine Research Technician | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Marine Research Technician Gulfport, MS, United States | req5602 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/19/2026 Back to Search Marine Research Technician The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Marine Research Technician. Position Details Location Marine Research Center 1030 West Pier, Port of Gulfport Gulfport, Mississippi, 39501 United States Division Ocean Enterprise Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Salary Range 48,177.00 - 65,180.00 Pay Grade E10 Posting Close Date October 30, 2026 Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The Marine\nResearch Technician performs specialized technical and research activities\nsupporting the testing and evaluation of uncrewed systems, autonomous and\nsemi-autonomous platforms, ocean-observing technologies, and associated\ninstrumentation at the Roger F. Wicker Center for Ocean Enterprise. The\nposition is responsible for configuring, operating, troubleshooting, and\nevaluating marine systems and oceanographic instrumentation used in research,\ndevelopment, testing, and evaluation activities. The position plans and\ncoordinates assigned field and vessel operations, evaluates equipment readiness\nprior to deployment, collects and analyzes research and system-performance\ndata, and prepares technical reports and research materials. The Marine\nResearch Technician applies technical knowledge and judgment to identify\nequipment and system issues, determine appropriate troubleshooting actions, and\nsupport successful field operations. The position works collaboratively with\nproject directors, principal investigators, University researchers, and\nexternal research partners. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Configures, installs, operates, and troubleshoots oceanographic sensors, instrumentation, uncrewed systems, and autonomous and semi-autonomous marine vehicles used in research, testing, and field operations. Plans and coordinates assigned boat and field operations, including determining equipment and instrumentation needs, preparing systems for deployment, and coordinating technical activities necessary to support testing and data collection. Evaluates the calibration, operational condition, and readiness of oceanographic equipment and instrumentation prior to deployment. Identifies equipment or system deficiencies and determines appropriate troubleshooting or corrective actions within established technical standards and procedures. Operates uncrewed systems and remotely operated vehicles during testing and extended field operations. Monitors system performance and makes technical adjustments necessary to support safe and effective operations within established protocols. Collects, processes, analyzes, and interprets data generated through research, experiments, testing, and field activities. Identifies performance issues, trends, anomalies, and other findings requiring additional review or testing. Analyzes system-performance results and prepares technical reports, research documentation, figures, tables, and other materials supporting scientific studies, publications, project deliverables, and research activities. Provides technical findings and recommendations to project directors, principal investigators, researchers, and other project personnel. Maintains assigned oceanographic equipment, instrumentation, tools, and technical resources and collaborates with University researchers, technical professionals, and external research partners in support of research, testing, and evaluation activities. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Associate's\ndegree or technical certificate from an accredited college or university in\nHydrography, Electronics Technology, Marine Technology, or a related field and\ntwo years of related experience involving oceanographic instrumentation, marine\nsystems, electronics, research testing, or evaluation. Must have United States\ncitizenship and a valid driver's license. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge of research methods, technical procedures, and practices associated with ocean technology research, testing, and evaluation activities. Ability to read, analyze, and interpret research publications, professional journals, technical procedures, and other technical documentation. Ability to organize and maintain tools, equipment, instrumentation, and other technical resources used in research and field activities. Skill in preparing technical reports, research documentation, maps, tables, figures, and other illustrative materials. Ability to communicate technical information effectively and respond to questions from researchers, technical professionals, customers, and other stakeholders. Ability to work collaboratively with project directors, principal investigators, University researchers, and external research partners. Ability to travel and perform fieldwork aboard large and small vessels. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's\ndegree in a related technical or scientific field. Two to four years of\nexperience working with ocean-observing instrumentation, electronic systems and\ncircuits, uncrewed marine systems, or research testing and evaluation\nactivities. Experience conducting field operations aboard research or other\nmarine vessels. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5648?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics Stennis Space Center, MS, United States | req5648 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics. Position Details Location John C. Stennis Space Center 1020 Balch Blvd Stennis Space Center, Mississippi, 39529 United States Division School of Ocean Science and Engineering Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Salary Range 68,000.00 - 71,000.00 Pay Grade Posting Close Date January 31, 2027 Special Instructions to Applicants Please apply through the USM Cornerstone portal and submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement (up to 3 pages), teaching philosophy statement (up to 2 pages), and contact information for three professional references. The application deadline is January 31, 2027. The expected salary for the nine-month period ranges from $68,000 to $71,000, with the potential for additional summer salary through grant-funded projects. For questions or additional information about the position, please contact the search committee chair at Anand.Hiroji@usm.edu. Any offer of employment is contingent upon satisfactory completion of a background check. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the\nUnited States on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The\nUniversity will not sponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). Job Summary The School of Ocean Science and Engineering seeks a full-time (nine-month) tenure-track Assistant Professor in Marine Geology & Geophysics to advance research and teaching in geological oceanography, hydrographic science, and seafloor science. The successful candidate will develop an externally funded research program in areas such as marine sediment properties and transport, seafloor mapping and exploration, and/or marine acoustic methods. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentor students in Marine Science and Hydrography, and collaborate with faculty and the Hydrographic Science Research Center. The university’s autonomous surface and underwater vehicles, ROVs, acoustic systems, and sediment characterization instruments offer opportunities for field research and interdisciplinary work. This position is based at Stennis Space Center, Mississippi, offering close proximity to and opportunities for collaboration with federal agencies, including NOAA’s National Data Buoy Center, the Naval Meteorology and Oceanography Command and its subordinate commands, and the U.S. Naval Research Laboratory. Primary Job Duties and Responsibilities Establish an independent research program in marine geology and geophysics, publish scholarly work, and seek external support. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in areas appropriate to the candidate's expertise and departmental needs, including possible contributions to ocean acoustics, hydrographic science, and seafloor science. Mentor and supervise graduate research, including students in Marine Science and Hydrography. Develop interdisciplinary collaborations within the School, the Hydrographic Science Research Center, and external research communities. Contribute to field programs, data collection and analysis, and responsible use of relevant research facilities and instruments as appropriate to the research program. Participate in service to the School, University, and profession, and perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Earned PhD in marine geology, geophysics, geological oceanography, marine science, hydrography, or a closely related field, by the appointment start date. Evidence of research productivity and potential to develop an externally funded research program, consistent with career level. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities (KSA) Knowledge of marine geology and geophysics, including seafloor and subseafloor processes, marine sediments, and field or analytical methods relevant to the candidate’s research and teaching. Ability to design and conduct original research, analyze and interpret marine data, publish results, and develop competitive proposals for external funding. Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate students, develop course materials, assess learning, and mentor student research in marine science and related fields. Skill in communicating research and technical concepts clearly in writing, presentations, classroom instruction, and collaborative settings. Ability to work with interdisciplinary teams and use appropriate acoustic, geospatial, autonomous, or sediment characterization methods for the candidate’s research program. Ability to participate in fieldwork, including occasional travel or overnight stays, as required by research activities and assigned teaching. Preferred Qualifications Research experience in marine sediment and seafloor properties or movement, seafloor mapping or exploration, underwater positioning techniques, marine acoustics, or related methods. Experience with multibeam, side scan, subbottom, underwater positioning systems or other acoustic systems; autonomous underwater vehicles; or sediment characterization instruments. Record of, or strong potential for, interdisciplinary collaboration, graduate mentorship, and development of external proposals, including post-doctoral fellowships. Ability to contribute to both geological oceanography and hydrographic science curricula. Ability to teach and mentor students in areas relevant to the position. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5635?c=usm": {
      "title": "Laboratory Coordinator",
      "description": "Laboratory Coordinator | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Laboratory Coordinator Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5635 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Laboratory Coordinator The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Laboratory Coordinator. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security Position Type Staff Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade E09 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must complete an online application\nat https://jobs.usm.edu. Along with the application, applicants should\ninclude: (1) a cover letter describing relevant laboratory experience and\nqualifications for the position; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement\ndescribing their experience with laboratory instruction, laboratory management,\nand laboratory safety; (4) a list of three professional references with contact\ninformation for each; and (5) unofficial transcripts from undergraduate and graduate\ninstitutions. Official transcripts will be required prior to a campus\ninterview. To receive priority consideration, applications must be\nreceived by November 22, 2026. The position will remain open until filled.\nApplicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a\nfull-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not\nsponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). The anticipated start date for\nthis position is August 2027. Direct inquiries to: Richard Swearengin, Chair School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science and Security The University of Southern Mississippi 118 College Drive #5127, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Richard.swearengin@usm.edu Job Summary This position is designed to oversee\nthe orderly, safe, and proper coordination and administration of forensic\nscience teaching and research laboratories. Primary Job Duties and\nResponsibilities Procures, maintains, and assembles materials, specimens and equipment for forensic science laboratories. Prepares instructional materials and lab manuals for forensic laboratories. Ensures laboratory workspace is prepared and clean prior to each lab. Works with faculty and staff to identify and maintain laboratory inventory lists. Assists University personnel and state auditors in identifying and locating inventory. Monitors lab teaching schedules in coordination with faculty and school director and instructs lab sections, as needed. Serves as a liaison with university safety representatives and ensures that lab safety requirements are adhered to within forensic laboratories. . Works with faculty to maintain laboratory equipment; obtain quotes and necessary repairs. Assists with lab fee account reports, budget decisions. Assists with student advisement, as needed. Performs other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s\ndegree from an accredited college or university in forensic science or related\nscience field. One-year related experience and/or training. Experience with\nMicrosoft Word and Excel. Experience with microscopes, balances, centrifuges,\nfume hoods, refrigerators, freezers, and other forensic laboratory equipment. Knowledge,\nSkills, and Abilities (KSA) Utilizes and maintains personal computer, microscopes, balances, centrifuges, and hand tools (hammers, screwdrivers, and pliers), refrigerators, freezers, and other forensic laboratory equipment. Ability to use standard office equipment such as stapler, staple remover, and scissors. Knowledge and competency of general forensic techniques. Understanding of safety guidelines and how to establish safety standards for a forensic laboratory. Ability to develop a detailed budget and account reports. Preferred\nQualifications Lab instruction, planning and set-up.\nSupervisory and training experience. Purchasing and budget experience.\nExperience developing and facilitating safety precautions within a laboratory\nenvironment. Experience within a forensic laboratory. Experience with financial\nsystems, purchase orders, and purchasing procedures. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5634?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Forensic Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Forensic Science | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Forensic Science Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5634 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Forensic Science The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Professor of Forensic Science. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special\nInstructions to Applicants Applicants must complete an online application\nat https://jobs.usm.edu. Along with the application, applicants should\ninclude: (1) a cover letter detailing potential for high-quality research and\nteaching; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of teaching philosophy and\ngoals; (4) a list of three professional references along with contact\ninformation for each; and (5) unofficial transcripts from undergraduate and\ngraduate institutions. Official transcripts will be required prior to a\ncampus interview. To receive priority consideration, applications must be\nreceived by November 15, 2026. The position will remain open until filled.\nApplicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States on a\nfull-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not\nsponsor applicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). The anticipated start date for\nthis position is August 2027. Direct inquiries to: Dr. Kuppareddi Balamurugan, Search Committee Co-Chair School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science and Security The University of Southern Mississippi 118 College Drive #5127, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Kuppareddi.Balamurugan@usm.edu 601-266-6048 Job\nSummary The School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science and Security at The University\nof Southern Mississippi invites applications for a full-time, nine-month,\ntenure-track Assistant Professor of Forensic Science. The candidate will\nbe expected to engage in teaching, research, service, and professional\nactivities; contribute to the growth and development of the school, college and\nuniversity; participate in student recruitment and retention efforts; serve on\nschool, college, and university committees; and advise and mentor\nstudents. This position is based on the Hattiesburg campus. The School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science and Security (CJFSS) is an\ninterdisciplinary school with more than 500 students pursuing undergraduate\ndegrees in Crime Scene Investigation, Forensic Biology, Forensic Chemistry, and\nCriminal Justice. The school offers undergraduate minors in Forensic Science,\nCriminal Justice, and Security Studies, as well as a fully online graduate\nprogram in Criminal Justice. Housed in the newly renovated Paul H. \"Bud\" Holmes Criminal Justice\nBuilding, CJFSS features more than 30,000 square feet of state-of-the-art\nteaching and research laboratories, modern classrooms, a computer laboratory,\nfaculty and student lounges, and a beautifully designed, fully functional mock\ncourtroom. The Crime Scene Investigation, Forensic Biology, and Forensic Chemistry degree\nprograms prepare students to apply a wide range of forensic techniques in all\nforensic settings. Students gain knowledge and hands-on experience in drug\nidentification and toxicological studies, serology and DNA analysis,\nfingerprint analysis, crime scene documentation, firearms identification, arson\nand explosives investigation, forensic photography, courtroom testimony\ntechniques, footwear analysis and other specialized areas of forensic science. Primary Duties and Responsibilities Deliver\nundergraduate forensic science lectures and laboratories via both online and\nface-to-face instruction. Advise\nand mentor undergraduate students on their degree progress and career\npreparation. Prepare\nand submit syllabi, grades, and official course documentation in a timely\nmanner. Actively participate in curriculum and course development. Conduct\nscholarly, funded research and publish findings in peer-reviewed outlets. Supervise\nand collaborate with undergraduate students on research initiatives. Engage\nin continuous scholarship and professional development commensurate with\nschool, college, and university expectations. Participate\nactively in the FEPAC (Forensic Science Education Programs Accreditation Commission)\naccreditation and re-accreditation process. Build\nand maintain professional relationships with local, regional, and national\nforensic laboratories and law enforcement agencies. Attend\nschool, college, and university meetings, committees, and recruitment events. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Forensic Science, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Biology or a closely\nrelated discipline from an accredited university. If Ph.D. is pending,\ndegree must be completed within first year of appointment. Demonstrated potential for undergraduate and graduate teaching. Demonstrated potential to establish a research program and acquire external\nfunding. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with students, faculty, and staff. Knowledge,\nSkills & Abilities Specialized\nknowledge in the field of forensic science as demonstrated through\ngraduate-level coursework, scholarly deliverables, and/or professional\nexperience. Ability\nto teach effectively (both online and in traditional settings) at the\nuniversity level in an undergraduate forensic science degree program. Ability\nto effectively engage in scholarship/professional development and service\nactivities. Ability to effectively advise and mentor students. Strong\nwritten and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Minimum two years of work experience in forensic science or related field. Ability to teach two or more undergraduate courses related to forensic\ndisciplines such as Forensic Biology, Forensic Chemistry, Forensic Quality\nManagement, Digital Forensics, Death Investigation, Crime Scene Investigation\nand Reconstruction, and other forensic-related areas. Work experience involving chain-of-custody, proficiency testing, laboratory\nanalysis of evidence, and courtroom testimony. Experience in maintaining and troubleshooting scientific instruments used in\nforensic laboratories. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    },
    "https://usm.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/5633?c=usm": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor Hattiesburg, MS, United States | req5633 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/27/2026 Back to Search Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor The University of Southern Mississippi is currently accepting applications for the position of Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor. Position Details Location Hattiesburg 118 College Drive Hattiesburg, Mississippi, 39406-0001 United States Division School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security Position Type Faculty Employment Status Full Time Grant Funded No Pay Grade Generic Grade Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Special\nInstructions to Applicants Applicants must complete an online application\nat https://jobs.usm.edu. Along with the application, applicants should\ninclude: (1) a cover letter detailing potential for high-quality\nteaching, scholarship/professional development, and service activities;\n(2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of teaching philosophy and goals; (4) a\nlist of three professional references along with contact information for each;\nand (5) unofficial transcripts from undergraduate and graduate\ninstitutions. Official transcripts will be required prior to a campus\ninterview. To receive priority consideration, applications must be\nreceived by November 22. The position will remain open until filled. Applicants\nmust be currently authorized to work in the United States on a full-time basis\nwithout the need for visa sponsorship. The University will not sponsor\napplicants for work visas (e.g., H-1B). The anticipated start date for this\nposition is August 2027. Direct inquiries to: Dr. Ragan Downey, Search Committee Chair School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security The University of Southern Mississippi 118 College Drive #5127, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 ragan.downey@usm.edu Job\nSummary The School of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security at The\nUniversity of Southern Mississippi invites applications for a full-time,\nnine-month, non-tenure-track Assistant Teaching Professor or Instructor of\nCriminal Justice. The teaching load is four courses per semester (fall and\nspring). This position is based on the Hattiesburg campus, and the candidate\nmay be asked to teach both in-person and/or online courses. The ideal candidate\nfor this position would be qualified to teach a wide range of criminal justice\ncourses. The\nSchool of Criminal Justice, Forensic Science, and Security (CJFSS) is an\ninterdisciplinary school with more than 500 students pursuing undergraduate\ndegrees in Criminal Justice, Crime Scene Investigation, Forensic Biology, and\nForensic Chemistry. The school offers undergraduate minors in Criminal Justice,\nSecurity Studies, and Forensic Science, as well as a fully online graduate\nprogram in Criminal Justice. Housed in the newly renovated Paul H.\n\"Bud\" Holmes Criminal Justice Building, CJFSS features more than\n30,000 square feet of state-of-the-art teaching and research laboratories,\nmodern classrooms, a computer laboratory, faculty and student lounges, and a\nfully functional mock courtroom. Primary\nDuties and Responsibilities Teaching,\nadvising, and mentoring undergraduate students. Online\nand face-to-face course delivery and instruction. Preparing\nand submitting syllabi, grades, and other required course documentation in a\ntimely manner. Engaging\nin scholarship/professional development and service activities commensurate\nwith school, college, and university expectations. Attending\nschool, college, and university meetings and recruitment events. Performing\nother duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Instructor of Criminal Justice Earned master’s degree in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or\nSecurity Studies (or a closely related field) from an accredited university or\nearned Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree from a law school approved by the American\nBar Association (ABA) and housed within an accredited university. Ability to teach effectively (both online and in traditional settings) at the\nuniversity level in an undergraduate criminal justice degree program. Ability to effectively engage in scholarship/professional\ndevelopment and service activities in the discipline. Ability to effectively advise and mentor college students at the university level. Strong written and oral communication skills. Minimum Qualifications:Assistant Teaching Professor of\nCriminal Justice Earned doctorate (Ph.D.) in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or Security\nStudies (or a closely related field) from an accredited university. ABD\ncandidates will be considered, but it is expected that the Ph.D. will be earned\nby the date of appointment. A J.D. degree alone (without also having an earned\nPh.D.) does not meet minimum qualifications for the Assistant Teaching\nProfessor position. Ability to teach effectively (both online and in traditional settings) at the\nuniversity level in undergraduate and graduate criminal justice degree\nprograms. Ability to effectively engage in scholarship/professional\ndevelopment and service activities in the discipline. Ability to effectively advise and mentor college students at the university\nlevel. Strong written and oral communications skills. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Specialized\nknowledge in the field of criminal justice, criminology, or security studies\n(or a closely related field) as demonstrated through graduate-level coursework\nand/or professional experience. Ability\nto teach effectively (both online and in traditional settings) at the\nuniversity level in an undergraduate criminal justice degree program. Ability\nto articulate concepts and instructions effectively to students. Ability\nto effectively engage in scholarship/professional development and service\nactivities. Ability\nto effectively advise and mentor students. Strong\nwritten and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Instructor of Criminal\nJustice Ability to effectively teach a wide range of criminal\njustice courses. Teaching, mentoring, and service experience at the undergraduate level. Experience with online course delivery and instruction using Canvas or similar\nlearning management systems (LMS). Professional experience in criminal justice, security, law, or other related\nfield. Demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially with students, staff,\nand faculty from diverse backgrounds, programs, and colleges. Preferred Qualifications: Assistant Teaching\nProfessor of Criminal Justice Ability to effectively teach a wide range of criminal\njustice courses at the undergraduate and graduate level. Teaching, mentoring, and service experience at the\nuniversity level. Experience with online course delivery and instruction using Canvas or similar\nlearning management systems (LMS). Professional experience in criminal justice, security, law,\nor other related field. Demonstrated ability to work effectively and collegially\nwith students, staff, and faculty from diverse backgrounds, programs, and\ncolleges. About The University of Southern Mississippi Since our founding in 1910, The University of Southern\nMississippi has remained dedicated to preparing\nstudents for success. We deliver programs to more than 13,000 students\nin Hattiesburg and Long Beach, at teaching and research sites across the\nMississippi Gulf Coast, as well as online. We\nare a community-engaged Carnegie R1 university, earning distinction as one of\nthe nation's leading research institutions. Southern Miss is known for pioneering work in\npolymer science, ocean science, spectator sports safety and security, and bringing\nlanguage to children with communication disorders. We are also a national\nleader in a broad range of disciplines, including cybersecurity, hydrography,\nnutrition, aquaculture, kinesiology, and economic development, among others. We produce graduates ready to enter\nfields that are leading the way in emerging technologies through programs such\nas computer engineering, information technology, and ocean engineering. We’re developing the next generation of\nbusiness leaders, while also responding to critical workforce shortages by\nproducing skilled professionals in education and nursing. With a tradition of\nexcellence in the arts, we are one of only 36 public institutions in the nation\naccredited in four major areas of the arts. Home to the Golden Eagles, our\nstudent-athletes compete in 17 NCAA Division I sports. With a culture marked by passion and\npersistence, Southern Miss is delivering graduates who are ready for life. For more information, visit www.usm.edu . The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment. EOE/VETS/DISABILITY. Apply Now Share Save Job Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Southern Mississippi is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or genetic information. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities in employment, its service, programs, and activities. If reasonable accomodation is needed to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and/or to receive other beneifts and privileges of employment, please contact: The Office of Equal Employment Opportunity & Labor Regulatory Compliance eeo@usm.edu McLemore Hall (MCL) 310 | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 Phone: 601.266.4050 | Fax: 601.266.4541 | TTY/TDD: 1.800.582.2233 www.usm.edu/equal-employment Employment Notices Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Click on the links below to learn more about employer requirements and your right to work: E-Verify Poster Right to Work Poster Employee Rights - Family and Medical Leave Act Employee Rights - Employee Polygraph Protection Act If you have questions, please email the Human Resources department at jobs@usm.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The\n University of Southern Mississippi is committed to the safety and \nsecurity of the USM community. The annual federal required compliance \ndocument which contains policy statements and crime statistics for the \nUniversity is available on the UPD website at http://www.usm.edu/police/ .\n You may also request a copy by contacting the University Police \nDepartment at Bond Hall, 1st Floor West, Hattiesburg, MS 39406 (601) 266-\n 4986. The policy statements address the University's policies, \nprocedures and programs concerning safety and security, for example, \npolicies for responding to emergency situations and sexual offenses. \nThree years' of statistics are included for certain types of crimes that\n were reported to have occurred on campus, in or on off-campus buildings\n or property owned or controlled by the University and on public \nproperty within or immediately adjacent to the campus. University Human Resources | 118 College Drive #5111 | Hattiesburg, MS 39406 USM Home Page | USM HR | About Hattiesburg if you have questions please email: jobs@usm.edu"
    }
  },
